ENTRY            14870   20260512   20260821   20260821       1530
SUBENT        14870001   20260512   20260821   20260821       1530
BIB                 12         47
TITLE      Level study of 14C via neutron scattering to the
           unbound levels of 13C
AUTHOR     (E.Saito)
REFERENCE  (T,SAITO,1993) Main reference.
           (J,NIM/A,349,544,1994) MACHO simulation code.
           #doi:10.1016/0168-9002(94)91224-6
INSTITUTE  (1USAOHO)
FACILITY   (VDGT,1USAOHO) The tandem Van de Graaff, beam swinger
           and TOF tunnel of the Ohio University Accelerator
           Laboratory.
INC-SOURCE (D-D)
DETECTOR   (SCIN) Three NE213 liquid scintillators, each 5 cm
           thick and 11.25 cm in diameter. Stilbene scintillator
           for flux monitoring.
SAMPLE     (6-C-13,ENR=0.98) 40.75 g of 13C powder in a 8.076 g
           thin-walled aluminum canister from Los Alamos
           National Laboratory.
           -------------------------------------------------------
           Isotopic Composition
           13C                              98 Atomic %
           12C                              2  Atomic %
           -------------------------------------------------------
           Gross Composition
           Total Carbon                     99.6 %
           Water                            <0.20 %
           Oxygen                           <0.15 %
           -------------------------------------------------------
METHOD     (TOF) First, measurement was performed at 0 degrees.
           Next, the actual scattering experiment with
           measurements made at 15,25,40,55,70,85,100,115,130,
           145, and approximately 158 degrees. The measurement
           of the 13C(n,n)13C cross sections involved
           subtracting a spectrum collected with the empty
           canister. Data were collected in five experiments
           over two years.
CORRECTION Finite geometry correction: The large size of the
           scattering sample required corrections for flux
           attenuation and a varying neutron flux across the
           sample. In addition, the experimental simulation code
           MACHO makes many experimental corrections. The data
           were corrected for air scattering, sample attenuation,
           sample contaminants, multiple scattering, secondary
           neutrons, and elastic and inelastic scattering from
           the sample of the neutron source continuum and
           contaminants.
STATUS     (APRVD) Approved by Dr. Th.Massey on May 14, 2026.
HISTORY    (20260512C) BP
ENDBIB              47
NOCOMMON             0          0
ENDSUBENT           50
SUBENT        14870002   20260512   20260821   20260821       1530
BIB                  5         18
REACTION   (6-C-13(N,EL)6-C-13,,DA)
ANALYSIS   After a peak has been kinematically identified, a TOF
           interval at least as wide as that for the elastic
           peak was selected. The on-line data analysis program
            (OLDA) reduced the counts in the peak interval to a
           cross section. R-matrix analysis was also used.
ERR-ANALYS (ERR-T) Uncertainties were added in quadrature's.
           (ERR-S,1.,5.) The counting measurements of the
           relative efficiency less 1%, and 5% for scattering
           measurements.
           (ERR-1) The energy dependence of both the 3H(p,n)3He
           and 2H(d,d)3He cross sections used in the
           relative efficiencies is known to 5%.
           (ERR-2) Uncertainty due to MACHO correction factor.
FLAG       (1.) May 1990 measurement.
           (2.) September 1990 measurement.
STATUS     (TABLE,,E.Saito,T,SAITO,1993) Table C.0. Data were
           provided by Th.Massey on May 12, 2026.
ENDBIB              18
COMMON               2          3
ERR-1      ERR-2
PER-CENT   PER-CENT
        5.0       13.0
ENDCOMMON            3
